    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [x] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version of the `main` branch\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.operators\n\n### Neovim version\n\n0.11.x\n\n### Description\n\nThere are a few edge cases related to text objects that have a width of zero, such as `i\"` when in front of `\"\"`. Now, the mini operators behave somewhat buggy as well when confronted with such zero-width text objects.\n\n### Reproduction\n\nGiven a buffer with this content:\n\n```lua \nlocal one = \"world\"\nlocal two = \"\"\n```\n\nWhile having `hello` in the register, run `gri\"` on the first line, and then run it once again on the second line.\n\n```lua\n-- expected result\nlocal one = \"hello\"\nlocal two = \"hello\"\n\n-- actual result\nlocal one = \"hello\"\nlocal two =hello \n```\n\n### Additional Context\nAs I myself only recently discovered, vanilla vim handles them somewhat inconsistently itself; `dib` will delete nothing, while `yib` will yank the quotes (effectively switching to `yab`). \n\nI can see zero-width textobjects in general being tricky in many regards (e.g., you cannot have an empty visual selection), and I do see that they are somewhat of an edge case. Nonetheless, I think it would be useful to handle them properly, since the behavior I expected would not only be more intuitive, but also useful to have for macros.",
