            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the issue!\n\nAs you pointed out, it is tricky to achieve with built-in textobjects. Mostly for technical reasons: operating on a textobject inside an operator works more or less like doing visual selection, *but* it is impossible to visually select *nothing*. There has to be a special case behavior of a textobject in Operator-pending mode for specific operators (like it is for `c`, for example), which making it built-in would be a visible breaking change (I'd imagine).\n\nThe good news is that 'mini.ai' has this behavior [out of the box](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/03387f53f1cb8c6a8ba0798041c6aa3525ed11ba/lua/mini/ai.lua#L1092-L1095) for \"replace\" operator of 'mini.operators'. If you enable 'mini.ai' (via `require('mini.ai').setup()`), then the use case you provide works as expected.\n\nClosing as both \"not really possible with built-in textobjects\" and \"already possible with 'mini.ai' textobjects\".",
